WEBVTT

00:00.260 --> 00:12.456
A score says a site is slow, not why. The Speed Engine shows why, and what to fix first. Shown on our deliberately slow demo site.

00:12.654 --> 00:19.329
The overview gives the four scores for the whole site: performance, accessibility, best practices and SEO.

00:19.520 --> 00:24.685
Down the side are the Core Web Vitals, the measurements Google uses. Red means poor.

00:24.875 --> 00:34.445
A short to-do list in plain words: who does each fix, how big the job is, and how much it helps.

00:34.636 --> 00:38.321
And one click makes a one-page summary to share with a client.

00:39.957 --> 00:48.802
On a slow phone the main content takes almost 50 seconds, and the engine's test agrees with Google's.

00:48.985 --> 00:55.302
The story in plain words: what holds the page up, and when visitors finally see it.

00:55.487 --> 01:04.801
The exact cause: a 3.7 MB main image, set to load late. Our team's own guide sits right below.

01:06.417 --> 01:10.775
Fix plans are written automatically after every audit. This one got eight.

01:10.951 --> 01:17.416
Each plan puts the fix next to its evidence, so a developer can act on it straight away.

01:17.569 --> 01:29.531
Every Monday it checks every Elite site, deep-audits the failing ones and posts results in Slack. Or just ask the Speed Bot.
